From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Pack the dense projections into one VMM range per GPU

NativeDense::load made one cudaMalloc per projection matrix (attention, GDN,
shared expert, the HC Q8 copies), and the driver backs each with whole 2 MiB
granules, so every matrix of a MiB or more lost up to 2 MiB to rounding
(upstream measured 2019 MiB costing 2400 on an RTX 5060).

The matrices now go end to end, 256-byte aligned, into one reserved virtual
range per NativeDense, and physical memory is mapped under them a granule at
a time, so a stage loses its payload plus less than one granule. The bytes
and the kernels are unchanged, so the output is the same. The VRAM saved goes
to the expert cache. A matrix the range cannot take falls back to cudaMalloc.

VmmRange made its chunks on, and granted access to, the device the driver
entry points were first resolved on. It now uses the device current at
reserve(), so a range on a layer split's later card lives on that card.

CUDA only (HIP keeps one hipMalloc per matrix). STRATA_DENSE_ARENA=0 turns
it off. The split search prices a later stage's matrices the same way.
---
